# NOTHING THAT IS NOT IN THE COMMIT, because `COPY . .` above copies the build context
# and the context is the repo root. An untracked file in the checkout lands in the image.
#
# Found via #366: the box carries five stale env backups from July, faucet.env.bak-*,
# none of which any pattern here excluded. Env backups are exactly the file most likely
# to hold an RPC password or the rate-limit salt, and an image layer is not a place to
# put one. I have NOT confirmed what those particular files contain, only that nothing
# stopped them being copied.
# THESE SIX MATCH THE ROOT OF THE CONTEXT ONLY, AND THAT IS NOT WHERE THE FILES ARE.
# Docker's `*` does not cross a `/`, so `*.env` matches `foo.env` and never
# `deploy/z3/faucet.env`. Added in #369 against exactly these files, and it excluded none
# of them. Verified by building a throwaway image with this context: `root.env` was
# dropped and all three of `deploy/z3/faucet.env{,.bak-*,.pre-*}` landed in `/app`.
#
# Kept anyway, because they are correct for anything that really does sit at the root.
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.env
env.*
*.pem
*.key

# THE ONES THAT DO THE WORK. `**/` is what reaches a nested path.
#
# This is not theoretical tidying. The RUNNING production image carries
# /app/deploy/z3/faucet.env with RATE_LIMIT_SALT and TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY in it, plus
# all five July backups. The image is local to the box and never pushed, so the exposure
# is bounded to someone who could already read the file, but a secret in an image layer
# survives the file being deleted and outlives whoever remembers it is there.
**/*.env
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.bak
**/*.bak-*
**/faucet.env
**/faucet.env.bak-*
**/faucet.env.pre-*

# The tracked template is documentation, carries no values, and the README points at it.
# Negation last: .dockerignore takes the LAST matching rule, so this has to follow the
# patterns it is exempting itself from.
!**/faucet.env.example
